1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,790
Two sills meet end to end.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:09,270
One piece drops in from above,
and now they can't slide apart.

3
00:00:09,633 --> 00:00:11,923
This is our first joint:

4
00:00:12,253 --> 00:00:14,363
the koshikake ari-tsugi.

5
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:22,697
It's a splice, a tsugite:

6
00:00:23,187 --> 00:00:27,697
a joint that joins two
members end to end, in line.

7
00:00:28,467 --> 00:00:32,648
A joint between members meeting
at an angle is called a shiguchi.

8
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:38,083
Ari is the dovetail:

9
00:00:38,473 --> 00:00:41,343
a shape that flares
out like a dove's tail.

10
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:44,423
Koshikake is the seat:

11
00:00:45,093 --> 00:00:47,363
the ledge the upper piece sits on.

12
00:00:48,153 --> 00:00:52,203
So this is a dovetail splice, with a seat.

13
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:56,193
You'll also see it called
shikimen ari-tsugi;

14
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:59,223
shikimen is another word for the seat.

15
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:08,203
It's used mainly to splice sills, the
timbers that sit on the foundation,

16
00:01:08,693 --> 00:01:13,603
and also ōbiki, the ground-floor
beams that carry the floor joists.

17
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,800
The building specification
behind Japan's Flat 35 home loans

18
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:24,160
names it as one of two splices
for sills, and says to keep

19
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:28,010
the splice away from posts
and underfloor vents.

20
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:32,770
It asks for sills at least a
hundred and five millimeters square.

21
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:36,430
The government's public-works
specification adds:

22
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:40,210
put the anchor bolt in the
upper piece, next to the joint.

23
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:45,717
There are two pieces.

24
00:01:46,367 --> 00:01:49,577
The male piece, ogi, carries the dovetail.

25
00:01:50,127 --> 00:01:54,197
The female piece, megi, carries
the socket and the seat.

26
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:56,617
Carpenters name the parts:

27
00:01:57,167 --> 00:02:03,297
the wide head, the narrow neck, and the
shoulder, where the neck meets the body.

28
00:02:07,467 --> 00:02:13,777
The dovetail and its socket take
roughly the upper half of the sill.

29
00:02:14,447 --> 00:02:16,497
The seat takes the lower half.

30
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:23,157
The male piece's lower half is
cut back, so it sits on that seat.

31
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,850
The male piece goes in from above.

32
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:33,340
The flare stops it coming
in from the end, and the

33
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:36,590
socket walls stop it
coming in from the side.

34
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:39,770
So it drops straight down:

35
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:46,410
the dovetail settles into its socket, and
the cut-back underside lands on the seat.

36
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:50,827
Once it's down, the flare
hooks the two pieces together,

37
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:55,057
so they can't simply slide
apart along their length.

38
00:02:55,107 --> 00:02:58,817
And the male piece rests on the seat.

39
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:00,533
How big?

40
00:03:00,913 --> 00:03:02,043
Proportions vary.

41
00:03:02,773 --> 00:03:06,103
One engineering study
works within these ranges:

42
00:03:06,553 --> 00:03:10,853
a neck a quarter to a third
of the sill's width, and a

43
00:03:10,893 --> 00:03:14,143
dovetail a quarter to a
half of the width long.

44
00:03:14,693 --> 00:03:16,663
Our drawing sits inside both.

45
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:22,640
In our drawing, each side slopes
about one in five, in line

46
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,470
with what the test reports
describe for these joints.

47
00:03:25,933 --> 00:03:30,433
In one training course, the
slope of the sides is set out

48
00:03:30,473 --> 00:03:34,803
with the sashigane itself,
using the width of its blade.

49
00:03:39,267 --> 00:03:41,837
Be clear about what it isn't.

50
00:03:41,947 --> 00:03:47,657
The hook holds only a modest pull,
so it's counted as weak in tension.

51
00:03:48,287 --> 00:03:51,317
One test report treats
it almost as a hinge,

52
00:03:51,567 --> 00:03:53,717
with very little resistance to bending.

53
00:03:54,447 --> 00:03:59,067
In a house, an anchor bolt holds the
upper piece down beside the joint.

54
00:03:59,107 --> 00:04:04,157
Never put the bolt in the female
piece, or inside the joint itself.

55
00:04:04,633 --> 00:04:06,703
And it fails suddenly:

56
00:04:07,493 --> 00:04:12,583
the female piece can split at the
notch, or the dovetail can shear off.

57
00:04:13,373 --> 00:04:16,453
Keep knots out of the
joint, and don't expect

58
00:04:16,493 --> 00:04:19,063
a longer dovetail to make it stronger.

59
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:28,150
Next time, we'll mark it out
and cut it, step by step.

60
00:04:29,067 --> 00:04:32,237
A dovetail above, a seat below.

61
00:04:32,887 --> 00:04:36,377
The upper piece, the
male, drops in from above.

62
00:04:37,067 --> 00:04:40,517
And the splice goes away
from posts and vents.
